\begin{abstract}
SIE-ASTRO v4.0 is a production-grade relativistic electromagnetic particle engine designed for high-throughput scientific computing environments. 
The framework integrates a fully parallelized relativistic Boris particle pusher, a constrained-transport (CT) Maxwell solver on a staggered Yee mesh, 
and an AES-256-GCM cryptographic ingestion pipeline for secure diagnostic frame transmission. 
The engine is implemented in C++20, leverages OpenMP for shared-memory parallelism, and uses OpenSSL for authenticated encryption. 
This paper presents the mathematical foundations, numerical methods, software architecture, and performance characteristics of the SIE-ASTRO v4.0 core.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
High-energy plasma simulations require numerical engines capable of evolving charged particle ensembles under relativistic electromagnetic fields while preserving divergence constraints and ensuring numerical stability. 
SIE-ASTRO v4.0 addresses these requirements through a modular architecture consisting of:
\begin{itemize}
    \item A relativistic Boris integrator for particle pushing,
    \item A constrained-transport Maxwell solver on a 3D staggered Yee grid,
    \item A secure AES-256-GCM ingestion pipeline for diagnostics,
    \item High-performance parallel execution using OpenMP.
\end{itemize}

